{0 Err_trace: typed errors with bounded tracing}

Full per-function reference: {{!module:Err}[Err]}.

{1 Overview}

{!module:Err} keeps recoverable failures typed while attaching optional,
bounded diagnostic provenance. A value has type [('a, 'e) Err.t]: its error
branch contains the original ['e] payload in {!Err.Error.t}, rather than a
string or global error registry.

Use {!Err.fail} where a component detects an expected failure. Use
{!Err.map_error} when crossing an error-domain boundary, and use
{!Err.mark_error} only for named import/export boundaries. Ordinary {!Err.bind}
and {!Err.map} propagation does not add trace noise.

A domain has one printer, so {!Err.Make} binds it once for the operations that
take one; {!Err.fail} and its siblings remain available for payload rows that
must stay open.

{1 Quick start}

{[
let parse value =
  Err.of_option ~pos:__POS__ `Missing value

let decode value =
  Err.map_error ~pos:__POS__ (fun error -> `Decode error)
    (parse value)
]}

The payload remains machine-readable: callers can match {!Err.Error.kind}.
The error wrapper retains the original detection origin and selected semantic
events, which can be rendered with {!Err.Error.pp} when a domain printer is
available.

{1 Runtime policy}

Choose a policy during process startup with {!Err.Config.set}:

- {!Err.Config.fast} records no events at all and captures no stacks: it
  disables the whole semantic trail, not only stack capture.
- {!Err.Config.deterministic} keeps the boundary trail and captures no stacks.
  This is the production preset for identical diagnostics on every backend.
- {!Err.Config.default} retains selected boundary events and an origin stack.
- {!Err.Config.debug} records every semantic action.

How much trail is kept and whether stacks are captured are independent axes;
the first ladder bundles them, and [deterministic] is the useful off-diagonal
combination.

{!Err.Config.with_config} installs a policy for one call and restores the
previous one even if that call raises. It is process-wide, not scoped to a
thread or domain.

Limits cap retained events, native frames, and imported stack bytes. Enabled
monitors still receive an event when [max_events] is zero. {!Err.Config.Never}
never automatically calls [Printexc.get_callstack]; an error detected without
[~pos] under that policy therefore has no origin at all.

{!Err.Config.make} and {!Err.Config.of_strings} return the library's own traced
result type with closed polymorphic-variant error languages. Configuration
failures can therefore be matched structurally or rendered with
{!Err.Config.pp_make_error} and {!Err.Config.pp_of_strings_error}.

{1 Boundaries and interoperability}

Use {!Err.protect} only to turn specifically classified exceptions into typed
errors. At an API that requires exceptions, {!Err.to_exn}, {!Err.export_exn},
and {!Err.or_raise} construct or raise a printable structured exception; most
code needs only [or_raise].

{!Err.import} lifts a third party's bare result and records [Import];
{!Err.export} records [Export] and then drops the wrapper, so a deliberate drop
goes through one named helper. {!Err.payload} is the same unwrap without the
marking, and {!Err.Error.pp_kind} renders a payload with no provenance for a
wire response or a user-facing message.

Adapters should use {!Err.Error.make_at} to import an externally captured
origin. {!Err.Stack.of_external} and [max_external_bytes] exist for a JavaScript
host that hands the program its own stack; other consumers can ignore them. The
base library has no third-party runtime dependencies.

Code that already holds {!Err.Error.t} uses {!Err.Error.map_kind} with
[~pos:__POS__] when crossing a typed error-domain boundary. The conversion
retains the original detection origin and records one [Map] event.

{1 Escaping a recursive walk}

A deeply recursive traversal cannot thread a result through every arm without
being rewritten around {!Err.bind}. {!Err.Escape.with_escape} establishes a
scoped exit and {!Err.Escape.throw} takes it, building the error wrapper at the
throw. Each call generates its own exception, so nested and concurrent uses
cannot catch one another. {!Err.Escape.or_throw} bridges an ordinary
result-returning call into such a walk.

{!Err.protect} is its companion for the opposite direction: absorbing an
exception raised by foreign code, rather than establishing an exit for the
current module's own recursion.

{1 Collecting every failure}

{!Err.List} stops at the first error. {!Err.Accum} runs every element and
reports every failure, which is what a validator over an untrusted document or a
compiler pass over a translation unit needs. Its failure is a list of
{!Err.Error.t}, so each problem keeps its own detection origin, and its result is
an ordinary {!Err.t} that still composes. {!Err.bind} semantics are unchanged.
